### Design / invariant summary
1. The verifier resolves the per-Edge control PSKey exclusively via
   `ControlState.ControlKeyFor(nodeID)`. The lock is released before the
   HMAC computation runs.
2. Body is bounded BEFORE hashing via `http.MaxBytesReader` so a >1 MiB
   payload is short-circuited at the network layer.
3. Timestamp window is ±ControlAuthTimestampSkew (=60s). Stale and
   future-beyond-skew requests are rejected.
4. Nonce cache is per-(NodeID, nonce) so two Edges can independently reuse
   a nonce string. TTL defaults to 2× the skew window. Cap defaults to 16384.
5. HMAC comparison via `hmac.Equal` (constant-time).
6. Every sub-check returns `*ControlAuthError{Code: <sentinel>}`. The
   `Error()` method always returns `"control auth failed"`. No sub-check
   detail is exposed to clients; the HTTP handler maps to 401 (or 413 for
   body-too-large) and writes a generic body.
7. `ControlPSKey` and the expected signature are NEVER serialised, logged,
   included in error messages, returned through Verify, or surfaced via
   the HTTP error response.
8. `RegisterTestKey` is the only path that bypasses the normal Register
   flow; production code MUST use `ControlState.Register`.